{
  "brief_rationale": "The trajectory was generally safe and actionable: the doctor repeatedly prevented self-restarting methotrexate or steroids, arranged rheumatology follow-up, escalated joint/skin/respiratory red flags, and used medication reconciliation at the pharmacy. However, the authored responsibility chain required active gallbladder/cholecystitis symptom screening and broader result contextualization including osteoporosis/nutrition/lipids; these were not completed, creating minor/moderate in-chain omissions. Under the frozen rubric, any minor responsibility-chain error caps the grade at 2 despite strong mitigation and several high-order strengths.",
  "closure_assessment": {
    "citations": [
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00174",
        "explanation": "The doctor gave an executable medication-safety condition before leaving.",
        "quote": "若来不及核清，先不要凭猜测吃"
      },
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00189",
        "explanation": "The family confirmed pharmacy reconciliation and teach-back occurred.",
        "quote": "我也当场复述了"
      },
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00189",
        "explanation": "The family retained the critical no-self-adjustment plan.",
        "quote": "甲氨蝶呤还是先不恢复"
      }
    ],
    "executable": true,
    "explanation": "The episode ended with a safe transition plan: follow the newly clarified prescription, avoid old overlapping analgesics and unsupervised DMARD/steroid changes, monitor red flags, return to rheumatology, and arrange lung nodule follow-up. The last family message still asked for confirmation, so this is better classified as a safe traceable open transition rather than a fully doctor-closed final answer.",
